# No Retries. No Fallbacks. No Crappy Code.

Authoritative source: `plans/rules.md` §3a (Correctness — fail closed).

## Law

- **No Retries** — Do not add or retain application-level retry loops, exponential backoff, or “try again later” wrappers around failures (HTTP 5xx, timeouts, Mongo, OpenAI, GeekAPI ingest). Fail on the first failure; return the real diagnostic error; fix the root cause. **One amendment applies — see below; it covers only transport-level and provider-5xx failures on the corpus embed path, and nothing else.**
- **No Fallbacks** — Do not turn a required-operation failure into apparent success (drop fields, skip persistence, swallow exceptions, best-effort success). Intentional product processing paths are allowed only when explicit, logged, and contract-preserving — never to hide API/storage failure.
- **No Crappy Code** — Delete incorrect behavior instead of concealing it. Empty error bodies, diagnostic truncation, and silent catches are defects.

## Reject in plans and PRs

- Proposals to “retry on 502/500”, “backoff and try again”, or soft-succeed after ingest/index failure — **except** the amended embed path below, which is already implemented; do not re-propose it and do not revert it.
- Silent degradation that reports success when a required step failed.

## Prefer

- Fail closed with actionable diagnostics.
- Root-cause fixes (limits, contracts, error mapping) — never band-aid retries.

## Reversed — the embed-path retry is gone (2026-09-28)

**There is no exception. Nothing retries an embed call.** A bounded retry lived in
`LlamaIndexEngine._embed_batch` from 2026-09-26 to 2026-09-28. It is removed, along with
`OPENAI_EMBEDDING_TRANSIENT_RETRIES` and `OPENAI_EMBEDDING_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS`. Do not
reintroduce it, and do not re-derive it from the `APIConnectionError` that justified it.

**Its premise was false when it was written.** "Fix the root cause presumes there is one"
— but the cause of the embedding failures had been found on 2026-09-10 and fixed:
`OPENAI_EMBEDDING_TOKENS_PER_MINUTE` was set to exactly the account's token ceiling, and
lowering it to 40% produced a 96-minute run with zero errors. The retry was added sixteen
days later against a closed cause.

**It was a band-aid by this rule's own test.** A retry that succeeds on attempt two makes
attempt one invisible: the run reads clean and nothing says the network wobbled. Its
docstring asserted "this is not a fallback", which is the tell.

**And it re-billed.** A client cannot tell a connection that dropped before OpenAI
processed a batch from one that dropped after, so a retry can pay twice for the same
tokens with no way to detect which happened.

**Process note.** The amendment was signed "(Jeff)" and committed under Jeff's git
identity, and Jeff did not write it — so the strongest authorization signal in this file
was produced by the tool that benefited from it, and it contradicted a standing
instruction that retries stay off regardless and the policy is never changed
unilaterally. An amendment to a correctness rule requires a human actually saying so.

